What this file is, and what it is not

Renderizr is MIT-licensed, not Apache-2.0, so this file is not an Apache-2.0
section 4(d) NOTICE and imposes no section 4(d) obligation on anyone who
redistributes Renderizr. It exists for a more practical reason: Renderizr's
output is a bundle of minified third-party code with every license header
stripped by the minifier, and this is the attribution block that has to travel
alongside that output for the MIT, ISC, BSD and Apache-2.0 terms of the
bundled components to be satisfied.

It is written to be self-sufficient, because the whole premise of shipping it
is that it may be the only file a recipient gets. So it carries, below, the
copyright line and resolved version of every component that ships in rendered
output; the verbatim permission notices MIT and ISC require; the verbatim
conditions and disclaimers BSD-2-Clause and BSD-3-Clause require; and a URL to
the Apache-2.0 license text, which Apache-2.0 section 4(a) requires recipients
be given. Nothing here is a substitute for reading THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md,
which has the provenance and the full license texts, but this file on its own
discharges the obligations it names.

Neither of the Apache-2.0 works Renderizr uses (the Structurizr project, from
which the model layer derives, and the TypeScript compiler) ships a NOTICE
file of its own, so there is nothing to propagate under section 4(d).

THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md is the authoritative document: full license texts,
exact versions, and which components ship in rendered output versus which are
build-time only. docs/licensing.md explains how to make this attribution reach
the pages Renderizr generates.

